:: Day5_Afternoon_A [day5 afternoon chloe mia jade]
<<nobr>>
<<centerImage "image/events/chloe_home.png">>
<<set $currentLocation to "events/chloe_home">>

<p>Chloe texted last night: "Mia and Jade coming over at 2! You HAVE to come. They're dying to meet you properly." Two of her best friends. I've met them briefly at a party once, a crowded room and shouted introductions and handshakes that lasted exactly two seconds each. Today that changes. Today I sit in a room with three women who've known each other since middle school and who speak a language made of inside jokes and shared memories and the kind of casual intimacy that comes from seeing each other through bad haircuts and worse boyfriends.</p>
<p>I knock on Chloe's door at ten past two because arriving exactly on time feels try-hard and arriving late feels disrespectful and the ten-minute window is the compromise my anxiety negotiated with my pride.</p>
<p>The door opens. Chloe is in a cropped hoodie and leggings, her hair in one of those messy topknots that takes twenty minutes to make look accidental. Behind her, I hear voices. Laughter. Music that's all bass and attitude.</p>
<p>"They're here! Come in, come in, come in." She pulls me inside. Wrist, not hand. That's becoming a pattern.</p>
<p>The apartment smells like vanilla candles and nail polish and white wine. Mia is on the floor, cross-legged, surrounded by a constellation of beauty products that covers the carpet like a cosmetic crime scene. She's small, blonde, with nails done in holographic lavender and an outfit that looks like it was assembled by someone who reads four fashion magazines simultaneously. She radiates the kind of effortless trendiness that makes you feel underdressed just by existing in her proximity.</p>
<p>Jade is on the couch. Tall, curly red hair cascading over bare shoulders, blue eyes that look at you like they've already decided three things about you and aren't telling you what any of them are. She's wearing a top that shows a lot of skin and doesn't care who notices. Her legs are crossed. A wine glass dangles from her fingers like an accessory rather than a vessel.</p>
<p>"So THIS is Alex," Mia says, and she stands up and circles me. Actually circles me. Like I'm a sculpture in a gallery. Her eyes sweep from my shoes to my hair and I can feel her cataloging every detail, the fit of my jeans, the way my t-shirt falls on my shoulders, the state of my skin and my hair and my eyebrows and probably the thread count of my socks.</p>
<p>"He's cute," Jade says from the couch. She hasn't moved. The wine glass hasn't moved. Her voice has the lazy authority of someone who's used to being listened to. "In a baby deer kind of way."</p>
<p>"Right?" Chloe slides onto the couch next to Jade. "That's what I said. Baby deer energy."</p>
<p>I'm standing in the middle of the room being discussed in the third person while all three of them look at me with varying degrees of evaluation and approval and hunger. Not sexual hunger. Something else. The hunger of people who see raw material and are already planning what to build.</p>
<p>"Sit down," Mia says, patting the floor next to her. "You're making me nervous standing there like a coat rack."</p>
<p>I sit. On the floor. Among the beauty products. A lip gloss rolls against my knee. A bottle of something called "Radiance Serum" leans against my thigh. I have entered the feminine ecosystem and I am surrounded.</p>
<p>They spend the next two hours pulling me into their world. Not forcibly. Not with threats or deals or Lily's particular brand of sweet extortion. They just include me. The way you'd include anyone who happened to be sitting in the circle.</p>
<p>Mia shows me her skincare routine. "Cleanser, toner, serum, moisturizer, SPF. Every morning. Non-negotiable." She holds up each product like a professor presenting slides. "Touch your face."</p>
<p>I touch my face.</p>
<p>"Oh my god." Her fingers replace mine on my cheek. Soft, clinical, assessing. "What do you use?"</p>
<p>"Uh. Soap?"</p>
<p>The room goes silent. All three of them stare at me. The silence has weight. It's the silence of heresy, of blasphemy, of someone admitting they've committed an unforgivable sin against the church of skincare.</p>
<p>"Soap," Mia repeats. The word sounds different when she says it. Like a diagnosis. Like a terminal condition.</p>
<p>"Just... regular soap. From the shower."</p>
<p>Jade sets down her wine glass. This is apparently serious enough to warrant putting down wine. "Chloe. Your boyfriend uses bar soap on his face."</p>
<p>"I know," Chloe says, and her voice has the patient suffering of a saint. "I know."</p>
<p>Mia is already squirting moisturizer onto her palm. "Come here. Face me. Close your eyes."</p>
<p>Her fingers on my face. Warm, sure, practiced. She rubs the moisturizer in small circles across my forehead, down my nose, over my cheeks, along my jaw. The cream is cool and then warm and it smells like cucumber and something botanical that I can't name. Her touch is different from Vanessa's corrections and Evelyn's adjustments. It's caring. It's tending. She's tending to me the way Sophia tends to her garden, with attention and patience and the unquestioned assumption that I'm worth the effort.</p>
<p>"You have really nice skin, actually," she says. "Good pores. Clear. You just need to take care of it." She rubs under my eyes. "A little puffy here. Do you sleep on your face?"</p>
<p>"Sometimes."</p>
<p>"Sleep on your back. And drink more water." She finishes the moisturizer and reaches for another bottle. "Jade, pass me the eye cream."</p>
<p>Jade tosses a tiny jar across the room. Mia catches it one-handed and dabs a minuscule amount under each of my eyes with her ring finger. "Always the ring finger," she says. "Lightest pressure. The skin under your eyes is the thinnest on your body. You don't want to stretch it."</p>
<p>I'm being taught the anatomy of face care by a twenty-year-old blonde on a floor covered in beauty products while my girlfriend watches with the satisfied expression of a conductor listening to her orchestra hit perfect pitch.</p>
<p>Mia doesn't stop. She's on a mission. A holy crusade to save my skin from the barbarism of bar soap.</p>
<p>"Okay, we need to talk about your pores." She leans close. Her face is inches from mine. I can smell her perfume, something sweet and citrusy that makes me think of orange groves and the kind of girls who visit them. "Open."</p>
<p>She's looking at my pores. My pores. The tiny holes in my face that I've never thought about once in nineteen years of having a face. She examines them with the focus of a diamond appraiser. I can see the reflection of my own face in her glasses.</p>
<p>"Not bad. But you need a toner. And an exfoliant. And do you even know what retinol is?"</p>
<p>"Is it a vitamin?"</p>
<p>She closes her eyes. Takes a breath. The breath of a saint. "We're starting from scratch. Jade, hand me the whole bag."</p>
<p>Jade tosses a canvas tote across the room. It lands at Mia's feet and opens like a fabric cornucopia, disgorging bottles and tubes and packets and sachets and devices that I can't identify. Mia begins laying them out on the carpet in order, left to right, creating a timeline of skincare that stretches from where I'm sitting to the edge of the coffee table.</p>
<p>"Step one: cleanser." She holds up a white bottle. "This is gentle. No sulfates. You massage it in for sixty seconds. Time it. People rush this step and their skin hates them for it."</p>
<p>"Step two: toner." A blue bottle. "Rebalances your pH. Your skin has a pH, Alex. It's like a pool. If the chemistry is off, everything goes to hell."</p>
<p>"Step three: serum." A dropper bottle with gold liquid. "This is hyaluronic acid. It pulls moisture from the air into your skin. It's basically magic."</p>
<p>She talks for fifteen minutes. I retain approximately eleven percent of the information. But she's holding my face and touching my skin and her fingers are soft and sure and there's something about being tended to by someone who knows exactly what they're doing that short-circuits the part of my brain responsible for resistance.</p>
<p>"You're going to do this every morning and every night," she says. "Non-negotiable."</p>
<p>"Both?"</p>
<p>"Both. A morning routine and a night routine. AM you need SPF. PM you need retinol. There's a system. I'll write it down." She pulls out her phone and starts typing. A text arrives on my phone thirty seconds later: a numbered list with product names and links.</p>
<p>Chloe watches all of this from the couch with the peaceful satisfaction of someone who has outsourced a task she didn't want to do herself. Teaching Alex skincare was always part of the plan. Mia just does it faster and with more enthusiasm than Chloe would have.</p>
<p>"He has really nice eyelashes," Mia says to Jade, tilting my chin upward. "Long. Thick. Naturally curled."</p>
<p>"Wasted on a boy," Jade agrees from the couch.</p>
<p>"Not wasted," Mia says. She looks at me with an expression that's half clinical and half something else. "Just... unrealized."</p>
<p>She releases my face. Hands me a sample packet of cleanser. "Start tonight. Your skin will thank me in a week."</p>
<p>I pocket the sample. Another item in the growing inventory of feminine things that I carry without choosing to carry. <<if $jamalPreIntro>>Jamal's card. <</if>><<if $zaraFlyer>>The Pulse flyer. <</if>><<if $hasNicolettesCard>>Nicolette's card. <</if>>And now a cleanser sample from a girl named Mia who thinks my eyelashes are unrealized.</p>
<<set $day5_miaSkincareExtended to true>>
<<set $miaSkincareGiven to true>>
<<set $skincareRoutineActive to true>>
<<addItem "Mia's cleanser sample">>
<<if $hairClipQuest>>
<p>Mia spots it immediately. Her eyes lock onto the sparkly pink butterfly like a missile tracking system acquiring a target.</p>
<p>"Oh my god. Oh my GOD. Is that a butterfly?" She reaches up and adjusts it in my hair, tilting it slightly to the left, and her face lights up like a sunrise. "Okay, you need to keep this forever. This is everything. Jade, look."</p>
<p>Jade looks. Nods. "It's giving main character energy."</p>
<p>"It's giving main character energy!" Mia repeats, louder, like she's confirming a biblical prophecy. She pulls out her phone. "Can I take a photo? Just of the clip. Your hair. The vibe."</p>
<p>Before I can answer she's already framing the shot, my hair and the butterfly clip filling her screen, and the shutter clicks and she saves it to her favorites and I've been documented. Again. Another photo in another woman's phone. Another image of me wearing something feminine, captured and stored in the growing archive of evidence that Alex is changing.</p>
<</if>>
<p>The afternoon unfolds in waves. Jade tells a story about a hookup at a club last month that involves a guy whose dick was "bent like a question mark, and honestly, same" and my ears burn so hot I'm surprised they don't set the couch on fire. They talk about men like they're selecting items from a menu. This one was too eager. That one was too rough. The one from Tinder lasted three minutes and then asked if she'd rate him on Google. They dissect male behavior with the surgical precision of people who've been studying the species for twenty years and have reached definitive conclusions.</p>
<p>And they include me. Not as a guy sitting awkwardly with his girlfriend's friends. Not as the boyfriend being tolerated. As one of them. They turn to me for opinions. They ask my thoughts on whether Jade should text the bartender back. They pull up his Instagram and we scroll through his photos together and I find myself saying "his jawline is okay but those shoes are a crime" and Mia high-fives me and Jade says "finally, a man with taste" and I realize I just evaluated another man's attractiveness out loud in a room full of women and nobody thought it was weird.</p>
<p>Mia evaluates my nails. Takes each hand, examines them, makes tutting sounds. "We need to do something about these cuticles. Do you have a file?"</p>
<p>"Like a nail file?"</p>
<p>"No, like a government file. Yes a nail file." She produces one from her bag of infinite supplies. "Give me your left hand."</p>
<p>She files my nails while Jade refills wine glasses and Chloe puts on a new playlist and the afternoon sun comes through the window in golden sheets and I sit on the floor having my nails shaped by a girl I met two hours ago while wearing a butterfly hair clip and smelling like cucumber moisturizer.</p>
<p>Jade decides I need better jeans. "These are dad jeans," she announces, gesturing at my legs with her wine glass. "You have a good ass. You need jeans that know it."</p>
<p>"I don't have a good..."</p>
<p>"You absolutely do." She looks at Chloe for confirmation. Chloe nods. "Next time we go to the mall, you're coming. I'm putting you in some proper denim."</p>
<p>Jade has been evaluating my body all afternoon. Not subtly. She sits on the couch with her wine and looks me up and down with the unhurried appraisal of someone who judges for a living. Which she sort of does. She works part-time at a boutique downtown, Chloe tells me, selling clothes to people who can afford to care about fit.</p>
<p>"Stand up," Jade says, mid-way through the afternoon.</p>
<p>I stand. Mia protests because she was in the middle of applying something to my forehead, but Jade overrules her with a wave of the wine glass.</p>
<p>"Turn around."</p>
<p>I turn around. My back is to three women. I can feel their eyes on me like heat lamps, concentrated, specific, covering every inch of my backside with professional-grade scrutiny.</p>
<p>"See what I mean?" Jade says to Chloe. "He's got a shape. But these jeans are hiding it. Straight cut. Relaxed fit. He might as well be wearing a garbage bag."</p>
<p>"They're just regular jeans," I say to the wall.</p>
<p>"Exactly. Regular jeans for a regular person. But you're not regular, Alex." I hear her stand. Her boots on the hardwood. She comes up behind me and does something that freezes every muscle in my body: she puts her hands on my hips and turns me slightly, adjusting my stance.</p>
<p>"See?" she says to the room. "When he stands with his weight shifted, you can see the line. Hips. Waist. It's there. Just buried under bad denim."</p>
<p>Her hands on my hips. Warm. Firm. Positioned the way Vanessa's were when she taught me the sway, but Jade isn't teaching me to walk. She's evaluating my body's potential. She's looking at me the way Nicolette looked at my hair: as raw material.</p>
<p>"Next mall trip, we're fixing this." She releases my hips. Goes back to the couch. Picks up her wine. "Skinny or slim fit. Dark wash. Maybe a high-rise." She looks at Chloe. "Is he ready for high-rise?"</p>
<p>"Getting there," Chloe says.</p>
<p>High-rise jeans. The kind that sit above the hip bones. The kind that women wear. The kind that would put the waistband of whatever panties I'm wearing directly against the top of the jeans, visible, undeniable, a lace border visible above dark denim.</p>
<p>"Trust me," Jade says, reading my expression. "You'll look amazing. Better than most of the girls I dress."</p>
<p>Better than most of the girls. She said it casually, like comparing a boy to girls is something she does every Tuesday. Like the comparison is obvious. Like anyone looking at me would place me in that category without thinking.</p>
<p>I sit back down. Mia resumes her skincare siege. Jade sips her wine and types something on her phone, probably ordering jeans in my size without telling me, because that's the kind of thing Jade does. Unilateral decisions about my wardrobe, made with the confidence of someone who has never been wrong about denim.</p>
<p>"You've got a good ass, Alex," Jade says, not looking up from her phone. "It would be a crime to keep it in dad jeans."</p>
<<set $day5_jadeJeansExtended to true>>
<<set $jadeMallTripQueued to true>>
<p>Mia, not to be outdone, pulls out her phone and starts saving makeup tutorials to my camera roll. Not asking. Saving. She takes my phone, opens YouTube, searches "natural makeup for beginners," bookmarks three videos, creates a folder called "just in case," and hands the phone back.</p>
<p>"Just in case," she says. The innocence in her voice is so thick it could be spread on toast.</p>
<p>By the time I leave, my face is moisturized, my nails have been filed and evaluated, Jade has decided I need skinny jeans, Mia has populated my phone with makeup content, and I've been <<if $hairClipQuest>>photographed with a butterfly clip and called "main character energy" by<<else>>fussed over, filed away and called "unrealized" by<</if>> two women I didn't know four hours ago.</p>
<p>I walk home in the late afternoon light. My face feels different. Softer. The moisturizer is still working, still sitting on my skin like a protective layer between me and the world.<<if $hairClipQuest>> The clip is still in my hair. I didn't take it off. Not because Lily's deal requires it. Because Mia said to keep it forever and Jade said it was giving main character energy and Chloe watched all of it with that conductor's smile and I forgot it was supposed to be embarrassing.<<else>> Chloe watched all of it with that conductor's smile, and somewhere in the last four hours I forgot it was supposed to be embarrassing.<</if>></p>
<p>I didn't agree to any of it. I didn't refuse any of it. I just got swept along in the current of three confident women who'd already decided what I was becoming.</p>
<p>The current is warm. The current is strong. And I'm starting to think that swimming against it takes more energy than I have.</p>

